Project Journal

Project Title

Smart Security System Using ESP32 and PIR Motion Sensor

Student

Prince Kumar

Total Project Duration

33 Hours

Project Description

The Smart Security System is an IoT-based hardware project developed using an ESP32 microcontroller and a PIR motion sensor. The system continuously monitors an area for human movement. Whenever motion is detected, the ESP32 activates a buzzer and an LED to provide an immediate alert. Because the ESP32 has built-in Wi-Fi, the project can also be extended to send notifications to a mobile phone. This project demonstrates the practical use of embedded systems, sensors, and IoT technology to create a simple, affordable, and reliable security solution.

---

Objectives

- Design a low-cost smart security system.
- Learn ESP32 programming and sensor interfacing.
- Detect motion using a PIR sensor.
- Activate an alarm using a buzzer and LED.
- Improve knowledge of IoT and embedded systems.

---

Components Used

- ESP32 Development Board
- PIR Motion Sensor (HC-SR501)
- Active Buzzer
- LED
- 220Ω Resistor
- Breadboard
- Jumper Wires
- USB Cable
- Power Supply

---

Methodology

Phase 1 – Planning and Research (9 Hours)

I researched different smart security systems and studied the working principle of PIR motion sensors. I selected the ESP32 because of its built-in Wi-Fi capability and good processing performance. I prepared a component list, designed the project workflow, and created a block diagram. The Arduino IDE was installed and configured successfully before beginning hardware implementation.

---

Phase 2 – Hardware Assembly and Testing (9 Hours)

All hardware components were assembled on a breadboard according to the circuit diagram. The PIR sensor, LED, and buzzer were connected to the ESP32. Each component was tested individually before integrating the complete circuit. Wiring errors were corrected, and the hardware setup was verified.

---

Phase 3 – Programming and Integration (8 Hours)

The Arduino program was developed using the Arduino IDE. The ESP32 continuously reads the PIR sensor output and activates the buzzer and LED whenever motion is detected. The code was tested several times, bugs were removed, and the system response was improved. Serial Monitor was used for debugging throughout development.

---

Phase 4 – Final Testing and Documentation (9 Hours)

The complete system was tested under different conditions to verify reliable motion detection. Sensor placement and delay settings were adjusted to improve accuracy. Final project photographs, documentation, and demonstration materials were prepared for submission.

